WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.495
After being assaulted, Fix finally stood up.

2
00:00:04.496 --> 00:00:09.165
He had an enormous bruise on his cheek, and his clothes were in tatters.

3
00:00:09.166 --> 00:00:15.785
Luckily, Aouda had escaped unharmed. Mr. Fogg was covered in dust, but he was fine.

4
00:00:15.786 --> 00:00:20.906
As soon as they were out of the crowd, Mr. Fogg thanked the detective.

5
00:00:20.907 --> 00:00:25.977
"No thanks are necessary," replied Fix. "But let us go!"

6
00:00:25.978 --> 00:00:28.181
"Where?" asked Aouda.

7
00:00:28.182 --> 00:00:33.191
"To a tailor!" He laughed, showing them his torn clothes.

8
00:00:33.192 --> 00:00:38.389
An hour later they returned to the hotel with Fix more suitably dressed.

9
00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:44.369
Passepartout was there, waiting for his master and armed with half a dozen six-barreled revolvers.

10
00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:52.757
When Passepartout saw Fix, he frowned. His expression changed, however, when Aouda explained their encounter with Colonel Proctor.

11
00:00:52.758 --> 00:00:57.108
"Fix seems to be keeping his word," Passepartout thought.

12
00:00:57.109 --> 00:01:01.346
At a quarter before six, the travelers reached the train station.

13
00:01:01.347 --> 00:01:09.235
It was already evening, cold and cheerless, and the sky was overcast. The train left the station on time.

14
00:01:09.236 --> 00:01:16.867
Even though it proceeded slowly, pausing at every stop along the line, the train kept Mr. Fogg and his companions on schedule.

15
00:01:16.868 --> 00:01:21.628
At eight o'clock, the steward entered the car and announced that it was time to go to bed.

16
00:01:21.629 --> 00:01:30.465
The backs of the seats were lowered, bedrolls were unpacked, and soon each traveler had a comfortable bed with clean sheets and a soft pillow.

17
00:01:30.466 --> 00:01:38.033
The next morning, after eating breakfast, Mr. Fogg and his companions watched the landscape of the vast prairies.

18
00:01:38.034 --> 00:01:43.150
Mountains lined the horizon, and creeks and rivers passed by their windows. Around

19
00:01:43.151 --> 00:01:49.112
noon the train approached a herd of ten—or twelve—thousand buffalo that were crossing the tracks. The

20
00:01:49.113 --> 00:01:55.051
train slowed, attempting to push through, but the mass of animals was too great. It

21
00:01:55.052 --> 00:01:59.971
took three full hours for the beasts to clear the tracks. By

22
00:01:59.972 --> 00:02:05.553
the time the last buffalo crossed the rails, the first had already reached the horizon. Passepartout

23
00:02:05.554 --> 00:02:10.769
was furious at the delay. "What a country!" he cried. "Mere

24
00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:16.627
cattle stop the trains. I would like to know if Mr. Fogg foresaw this delay!" While

25
00:02:16.628 --> 00:02:22.460
Passepartout was cursing impatiently, Aouda was growing anxious for a different reason. At

26
00:02:22.461 --> 00:02:27.514
the last stop, several passengers had walked up and down the platform for fresh air. Among

27
00:02:27.515 --> 00:02:31.050
them Aouda had recognized Colonel Proctor. Her

28
00:02:31.051 --> 00:02:36.130
heart pounded when she saw the man that Mr. Fogg had promised to challenge. It

29
00:02:36.131 --> 00:02:41.286
was necessary, at all costs, to prevent Mr. Fogg from seeing his adversary. Aouda

30
00:02:41.287 --> 00:02:46.438
seized a moment when Mr. Fogg was asleep to tell Fix and Passepartout. "Colonel

31
00:02:46.439 --> 00:02:50.235
Proctor on this train!" cried Fix. "Well,

32
00:02:50.236 --> 00:02:55.291
before he settles with Mr. Fogg, he has got to settle with me!" "Mr.

33
00:02:55.292 --> 00:03:00.334
Fix," replied Aouda, "Mr. Fogg will allow no one to avenge him. He

34
00:03:00.335 --> 00:03:07.730
told me that he was prepared to return to America after his journey to find this man. He must not see Colonel Proctor!" Mr.

35
00:03:07.731 --> 00:03:11.660
Fogg woke up, and the conversation quickly stopped. Aouda

36
00:03:11.661 --> 00:03:15.162
madly searched for a way to distract Mr. Fogg. Suddenly

37
00:03:15.163 --> 00:03:22.634
an idea came to her: whist! She whispered her idea to Fix, and soon after a game began. Fix

38
00:03:22.635 --> 00:03:30.025
knew the game well, and Aouda and Passepartout picked it up quickly. They played all morning. Shortly

39
00:03:30.026 --> 00:03:35.568
after lunch they had just begun a new round when the train let out a whistle and stopped. Passepartout

40
00:03:35.569 --> 00:03:40.613
put his head out the window but could see nothing that could have caused the delay. He

41
00:03:40.614 --> 00:03:45.302
jumped out of the car and found the signalman talking to the engineer. "No!

42
00:03:45.303 --> 00:03:48.691
You can't pass," the signalman was saying. "The

43
00:03:48.692 --> 00:03:52.487
bridge at Medicine Creek is shaky and can't bear the weight of the train! The

44
00:03:52.488 --> 00:03:55.565
river is deep there. It's not safe." The

45
00:03:55.566 --> 00:04:02.582
passengers would have to walk along the river to a safe place to cross and then walk another six hours to the next station. Passepartout's

46
00:04:02.583 --> 00:04:07.551
heart almost stopped when he heard this news. By

47
00:04:07.552 --> 00:04:13.478
now a crowd had gathered around the engineer and the signalman, and an argument broke out. Finally

48
00:04:13.479 --> 00:04:17.345
the engineer calmed the crowd and suggested a daring plan. By

49
00:04:17.346 --> 00:04:20.301
accelerating the train to its very highest speed, they

50
00:04:20.302 --> 00:04:26.717
might have a chance of racing over the bridge before it collapsed into the rapids below. Passepartout

51
00:04:26.718 --> 00:04:34.729
was shocked. He was ready to try almost anything to get over Medicine Creek, but this solution was almost a little too daring! He

52
00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:38.538
ran back to the cabin to tell the others what was happening. "All aboard!"

53
00:04:38.539 --> 00:04:42.427
cried the conductor. The engineer

54
00:04:42.428 --> 00:04:48.837
reversed the train for almost a mile, and then, with a whistle, the train began moving forward again. The engine

55
00:04:48.838 --> 00:04:55.023
puffed huge clouds of steam as the train gained speed, going faster and faster. The cars

56
00:04:55.024 --> 00:04:58.629
shook and the windows rattled. It was

57
00:04:58.630 --> 00:05:02.822
like a frightful iron beast, racing to its destruction. Just before

58
00:05:02.823 --> 00:05:07.091
the river, the train exceeded a hundred miles an hour. It sailed

59
00:05:07.092 --> 00:05:10.721
over the bridge, hardly touching the rails at all! The last

60
00:05:10.722 --> 00:05:21.968
car had just reached the other side safely when the bridge fell with a crash into the rapids below. Passepartout's Guidebook

61
00:05:21.969 --> 00:05:28.983
That was quite a train ride—being delayed by thousands of buffalo, speeding over a collapsing bridge . . . Whew!

62
00:05:28.984 --> 00:05:32.808
Aside from these incidents, our train journey has been quite enjoyable.

63
00:05:32.809 --> 00:05:38.649
We have been traveling over America's prairies, large areas of open land like fields or meadows.

64
00:05:38.650 --> 00:05:44.000
Tall grass and colorful wildflowers grow on the prairies, but there are very few trees.

65
00:05:44.001 --> 00:05:52.221
In rainy areas the grass grows very tall, so tall that you could ride a horse and no one would see you! Now that's tall!

66
00:05:52.222 --> 00:05:56.882
Many kinds of animals live here, including antelope, buffalo, and rabbits.

67
00:05:56.883 --> 00:06:01.741
Buffalo are the largest mammals in North America, standing close to six feet high.

68
00:06:01.742 --> 00:06:06.424
Even though they're big, they can run really fast, about 25 miles an hour.

69
00:06:06.425 --> 00:06:16.109
So don't tease a buffalo because it'll catch you. And buffalo have horns. Ouch!

